WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.440
The 1818 novel was revolutionary in

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:07.640
its depiction of science and religion and
served as a pioneer text in the sci-fi genre.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.640
Mary Shelley's Frankenstein.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.020
On the surface, it’s a novel about a scary
monster, but her sympathetic description of

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.819
a soulful creature makes us rethink who we label as the “monster.”

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.120
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka, and this is Monstrum.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.080
Remember, this is not Frankenstein.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.400
This is.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:36.739
Although Shelley’s character was not a doctor of any kind, he actually never even finished

00:00:36.739 --> 00:00:45.670
his university studies, I’ve decided to
refer to him as Dr. Frankenstein as he was

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:52.080
first called in the 1931 movie to emphasize
the distinction between him and his creation.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.460
Dr. Frankenstein was the Scientist who created the nameless creature.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.620
Boris Karloff’s depiction of a tall, shambling,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.610
mute man with bolts sticking out of his neck has become the classic image of the monster.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.860
But Shelley’s original description is actually much more macabre.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:15.240
Shelly writes, quote, “His yellow skin scarcely covered the work of muscles and arteries beneath;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.640
his hair was of a lustrous black, and flowing; his teeth of a pearly whiteness...his watery

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.360
eyes...his shriveled complexion, and straight black lips.”

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.630
Shelley’s Dr. Frankenstein assembled his

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.490
Creature from materials found in “charnel
houses,” the vaults where corpses were kept.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.670
But he also took bits from “the slaughter-house.”

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.500
So, yes, her Creature is made of both human and animal parts.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.560
But here’s the thing.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.460
The Creature definitely looks scary, but just because something looks scary, doesn’t mean it is.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.580
Shelley’s original creation is a highly
intelligent vegetarian who hates the idea

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.340
of harming another living creature.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.940
At the beginning of the novel, he wants nothing more than to be accepted and loved by another being.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.400
Ironically, it’s the monstrous treatment
he receives from humans that drives him to kill.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:08.000
Dr. Frankenstein abandons the Creature at the moment of his animation, letting what

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:13.690
basically amounts to a newborn wander into the darkness alone to fend for himself.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.040
The tension between Frankenstein and the Creature represents the struggle between parent and

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.080
child, self and other, love and hate, science and morality, abandonment and acceptance.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.140
It’s a warning, to treat all living things
with respect, or else.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:32.330
200 year old spoiler alert— Shelley’s
novel ends with Frankenstein and his entire

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.250
family dead, and the Creature himself presumably committing suicide.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.520
What led Mary Shelley to explore these dark and tragic themes?

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.040
...That’s complicated.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.670
Mary’s life was plagued by death.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:49.810
Her own mother died from delivery complications only a few days after giving birth.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.980
Shelley would mourn the woman she never knew for the rest of her life.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.480
Shelley’s first child with her married lover

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.220
Percy, died shortly after birth in 1815.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.490
The following year was known as the “Year Without a Summer”

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.680
due to the lingering effects of the eruption
of Mount Tambora.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.160
Stuck indoors, Shelley and her friends entered into a “dare” to pen the most terrifying

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.690
story they could imagine.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:15.480
Shelley’s story came to her in a nightmare.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.200
In 1818, she finished Frankenstein, and was finally able to get married—but only because

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.540
Percy’s pregnant wife committed suicide.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.470
Mary’s second child Clara, also died that
year, and their third child, William followed

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.760
Clara to the grave in 1819.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:36.360
Then, just four years later, in 1822, Percy’s
drowned body washed ashore.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.650
All that death and grief may be why when editing

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:43.810
Frankenstein for an 1831 edition, Shelley
made Dr. Frankenstein’s decision to form

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.180
the Creature a matter of “fate.”

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:52.580
This change makes the Creature more of a monster as his violence and desire to destroy Frankenstein becomes his “destiny.”

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.760
Shelley’s original text, and all those that

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:00.310
follow in its shadow, makes us ask, “Who
is the real monster in this story?”

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:05.040
We are constantly updating the Frankenstein myth and the themes of life, loss, and monstrosity

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:11.030
Shelley wove into the original text as a way to explore our definitions of humanity.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.870
Interpretations like Ex Machina explore the intimate, and complicated,&nbsp; relationship

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:21.340
between the creator and the created, and asks the audience to consider if robotic consciousness constitutes “life.”

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.800
You are dead center of the greatness scientific event in the history of man.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:34.220
If you've created a conscious machine, it's not the history of man, that's the history of gods.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.240
The film Splice, looks at how engineering

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:42.380
human and animal DNA to design an entirely new life form can result in dangerous, unforeseen

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.840
complications—even if the creator shelters, educates, and loves their creation.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:52.320
Do you think they could really look at this face, and see anything less than a miracle?

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.300
Frankenweenie, retells the Frankenstein storyas a young boy’s decision to resurrect his

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:02.120
dead dog, emphasizing the devastation, and
desperation, that comes from loss.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:08.180
Sparky! You're Alive! I can't believe it. You're alive!

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:13.340
The 2017 comic series Victor LaValle’s Destroyer updates Shelley’s story for the current

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:19.110
moment—adding important conversations about
race, gender, immigration, police brutality,

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:23.620
social injustice, and the proliferation of
violence in the modern world.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:25.650
All of these interpretations show sympathy

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:29.390
for both the creatures and their tragically
flawed creators.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:33.890
Despite all the destruction that comes from scientific experimentation in these adaptations,

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:39.300
I don’t believe Shelley intended her original story to scare readers into believing all

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.890
science is evil or monstrous.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:47.240
I do think she warns us to consider the repercussions of technological and scientific advancement.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.560
Just because we can, doesn’t mean we should.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.950
Shelley’s novel calls us to be accountable
for what we create, and what might be destroyed

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:55.680
in the process.

